WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.610
Hold your price, handle discounts, and check your numbers.

2
00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:08.170
This is the video for Module 3 of Pricing and Value Strategy.

3
00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:19.230
Many women calculate a good price, then give it away in the first minute. 'It is 70,000 naira, but I can reduce it' invites the client to ask for less.

4
00:00:19.580 --> 00:00:26.580
So say the price as a fact, link it to what is included, and wait. Silence is normal. The client is thinking.

5
00:00:27.830 --> 00:00:32.670
When a client asks for less, trade scope or terms instead of cutting your value.

6
00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:37.080
Write clear payment terms, and raise prices fairly.

7
00:00:38.330 --> 00:00:40.870
And check a few simple numbers every month.

8
00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:47.560
A discount does not come out of your whole price. It comes out of the small part above your floor.

9
00:00:48.810 --> 00:00:53.630
With no discount, Zainab earns 12,600 naira above her floor.

10
00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:58.700
A 5% discount leaves 9,100 naira.

11
00:00:59.950 --> 00:01:11.040
A 'small' 10% discount leaves only 5,600 naira. That removes more than half of her margin. And a 20% discount means she works at a loss.

12
00:01:12.290 --> 00:01:19.560
For example, a fashion brand in Kaduna asks Zainab, 'Can you do 50,000 naira?' She thanks them and stays calm.

13
00:01:20.810 --> 00:01:26.040
She checks. 50,000 naira is 7,400 naira below her floor.

14
00:01:27.290 --> 00:01:32.300
She asks which part matters most: the posts, the catalogue updates or the report.

15
00:01:33.550 --> 00:01:38.520
Option one is a smaller scope: the Basic package at 58,000 naira.

16
00:01:39.770 --> 00:01:52.810
Option two is better terms: Standard at 66,000 naira a month, for three months paid upfront. Still above her floor. The owner chooses option two, and Zainab confirms it in writing.

17
00:01:54.060 --> 00:02:08.200
Ask for something of value in return: less scope, a longer commitment, payment upfront or a longer deadline. If no option reaches your floor, decline politely. Losing a below-floor job is not a loss.

18
00:02:09.450 --> 00:02:19.140
For projects, take a deposit, for example 50%, and the balance before you release final files. Retainers are paid at the start of each month.

19
00:02:19.490 --> 00:02:30.130
Put a due date on every invoice, and say what happens if payment is late: a reminder, a second reminder after 3 days, and work paused after 7 days overdue.

20
00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:34.960
Always confirm payment in your own banking app, never from a screenshot alone.

21
00:02:36.210 --> 00:02:50.100
Costs rise and your skill grows, so prices must move too. For example, Zainab has served a pharmacy for 18 months at 60,000 naira. That is only 2,600 naira above her floor.

22
00:02:51.350 --> 00:02:55.580
She decides on a 15% increase, to 69,000 naira.

23
00:02:56.830 --> 00:03:00.320
She gives at least 30 days' notice, so the client can plan.

24
00:03:01.570 --> 00:03:06.950
She lists what has improved, and offers the lighter Basic package if the budget is tight.

25
00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:14.070
The pharmacy accepts. Her margin on this client rises to 11,600 naira.

26
00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:30.720
Track hours for every client, including messages and calls. Then calculate the effective hourly rate and the margin above floor. Zainab's Client A pays 70,000 naira, but the work took 18 hours. That is below her floor.

27
00:03:31.970 --> 00:03:37.640
Client D is still on an old price, 17,400 naira below her floor.

28
00:03:38.890 --> 00:03:50.690
And she billed only 64 of her 88 hours. With Client D on Basic and one more Standard client, her income would reach 366,000 naira, above her target.

29
00:03:51.040 --> 00:03:57.380
A big client can still be a poor client. Look at margin above floor, not just the size of the payment.

30
00:03:58.630 --> 00:04:05.540
Now build your discount table, write your payment terms, and track your hours for a month. Change one thing at a time.

31
00:04:05.890 --> 00:04:07.830
Continue with the lessons below.
